<td id="S5.T1.2.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.25pt;padding-bottom:1.25pt;"><math id="S5.T1.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.25pt;padding-bottom:1.25pt;"><math id="S5.T1.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>An illustration of the payoff matrix for agent 1 (represented as the row player) under the kind of policy generated by the procedure from the proof of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Here <math id="S5.T1.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\{a,b,c,d,e,f\},H=\{(1,b),(1,d),(2,a),(2,c),(2,d)\},m_{b,a}=1,m_{b,c}=2,m_{b,d}=1,m_{d,a}=2,m_{d,c}=1,m_{d,d}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\{a,b,c,d,e,f\},H=\{(1,b),(1,d),(2,a),(2,c),(2,d)\},m_{b,a}=1,m_{b,c}=2,m_{b,d}=1,m_{d,a}=2,m_{d,c}=1,m_{d,d}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The highlighted actions <math id="S5.T1.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> are depicted in gray. The available actions in a given scenario are not depicted.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s create a procedure for randomly generating policies such that the average error of the policies generated by this procedure is at most our bound. Let’s “highlight” some subset <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}\times A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}\times A</annotation></semantics></math> where each element is chosen independently with probability <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{\alpha}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{\alpha}{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. For every <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}\in A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,a_{1})\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,a_{1})\in H</annotation></semantics></math> and for every <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}\in A</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,a_{2})\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,a_{2})\in H</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{a_{1},a_{2}}\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{a_{1},a_{2}}\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly at random.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We can now define our policy <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>: if the agents play actions <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,a_{1}),(2,a_{2})\in H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,a_{1}),(2,a_{2})\in H</annotation></semantics></math> then agent <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{a_{1},a_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{a_{1},a_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> wins. If only one agent plays a highlighted action then that agent wins, and if neither agent plays a highlighted action then both win with probability <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that if both agents can access a highlighted action, the outcome will be determined by the sub-grid of highlighted actions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To compute the average error of <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we would naively generate a policy <math id="S5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> as described, sample an anonymous scenario from <math id="S5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, flip a coin to decide the order of the agents, and then compute the probability of the principal rewarding the wrong agent. Equivalently, though, we can sample an anonymous scenario and decide the ordering of the agents first, then highlight our actions, then decide which agent to favor if both play highlighted actions, then compute the probability of the principal rewarding the wrong agent. Let’s consider those last two steps together: suppose we fix the set of highlighted actions that the desired winner can access (say, <math id="S5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{w}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{w}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>) and we do the same for the desired loser (denoted <math id="S5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{l}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{l}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>). Let’s upper bound the probability that the desired loser wins by upper bounding the probability that the desired winner lacks a single highlighted action which guarantees a win against all of the desired loser’s highlighted actions. The probability that any one of the desired winner’s highlighted actions beats all of the desired loser’s highlighted actions is <math id="S5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{-|B_{l}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{-|B_{l}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the probability that every one of the desired winner’s highlighted actions fails to guarantee a win is <math id="S5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(1-2^{-|B_{l}|}\right)^{|B_{w}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(1-2^{-|B_{l}|}\right)^{|B_{w}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note by inspection that this formula continues to hold if any of <math id="S5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{w},B_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{w},B_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> are empty.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s now take another step back and consider the distribution of <math id="S5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B_{w}|,|B_{l}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B_{w}|,|B_{l}|</annotation></semantics></math> for a fixed anonymous scenario <math id="S5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed desired winner <math id="S5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. By our procedure for choosing <math id="S5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B_{w}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B_{w}|</annotation></semantics></math> follows a binomial distribution with <math id="S5.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{w}(s)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{w}(s)|</annotation></semantics></math> samples and a success probability of <math id="S5.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S5.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B_{l}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B_{l}|</annotation></semantics></math> follows a binomial distribution with <math id="S5.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{l}(s)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{l}(s)|</annotation></semantics></math> samples and a success probability of <math id="S5.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, <math id="S5.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B_{w}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B_{w}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|B_{l}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|B_{l}|</annotation></semantics></math> are independent. Thus, conditional on being in anonymous scenario <math id="S5.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (note that <math id="S5.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> doesn’t matter), the probability of the desired loser winning is upper bounded by <math id="S5.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{\begin{subarray}{c}X\sim B(|C_{w}(s)|,\beta)\\
Y\sim B(|C_{l}(s)|,\beta)\end{subarray}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{\begin{subarray}{c}X\sim B(|C_{w}(s)|,\beta)\\
Y\sim B(|C_{l}(s)|,\beta)\end{subarray}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally we can take the expectation over anonymous scenarios to see that some policy has error at most <math id="S5.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\underset{\begin{subarray}{c}X\sim B(|C_{w}(s)|,\beta)\\
Y\sim B(|C_{l}(s)|,\beta)\end{subarray}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\underset{\begin{subarray}{c}X\sim B(|C_{w}(s)|,\beta)\\
Y\sim B(|C_{l}(s)|,\beta)\end{subarray}}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">We know that <math id="S5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{w}(s)|&lt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{w}(s)|&lt;m</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{l}(s)|&gt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{l}(s)|&gt;n</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at most <math id="S5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, if we define <math id="S5.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S^{\prime}=\{s\in S:|C_{w}(s)|\geq m,|C_{l}(s)|\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≥</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}=\{s\in S:|C_{w}(s)|\geq m,|C_{l}(s)|\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math> then our bound becomes <math id="S5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\in S^{\prime}}{\sum}\left[P(s)\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]\right]+\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\in S^{\prime}}{\sum}\left[P(s)\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(1-2^{-Y}\right)^{X}\right]\right]+\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s now focus on the inner expectation and for every <math id="S5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,...,k\},j\in\{1,...,l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,...,k\},j\in\{1,...,l\}</annotation></semantics></math> let’s define the event <math id="S5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> to say <math id="S5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in[\mu_{i}\beta m,\mu_{i-1}\beta m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in[\mu_{i}\beta m,\mu_{i-1}\beta m)</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S5.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mu_{1}\beta m,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mu_{1}\beta m,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and to say <math id="S5.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in(\nu_{j-1}\log_{2}(r),\nu_{j}\log_{2}(r)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in(\nu_{j-1}\log_{2}(r),\nu_{j}\log_{2}(r)]</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S5.p8.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(-\infty,\nu_{1}\log_{2}(r)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-\infty,\nu_{1}\log_{2}(r)]</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Conditional on witnessing event <math id="S5.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> we have:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1-2^{-Y})^{X}\leq e^{-X2^{-Y}}\leq e^{-\mu_{i}\beta m2^{-\nu_{j}\log_{2}(r)}}=\theta_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>X</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1-2^{-Y})^{X}\leq e^{-X2^{-Y}}\leq e^{-\mu_{i}\beta m2^{-\nu_{j}\log_{2}(r)}}=\theta_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">Thus our inner expectation is bounded by:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sum_{j=1}^{l}\theta_{i,j}\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{P}}\left[E_{i,j}\right]+\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{P}}\left[\bigcap_{i,j}E_{i,j}^{c}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">X</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sum_{j=1}^{l}\theta_{i,j}\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{P}}\left[E_{i,j}\right]+\underset{X,Y}{\mathbb{P}}\left[\bigcap_{i,j}E_{i,j}^{c}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">A standard tail bounding argument shows that <math id="S5.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{X,Y}\left[E_{i,j}\right]\leq\zeta_{i-1}\xi_{j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{X,Y}\left[E_{i,j}\right]\leq\zeta_{i-1}\xi_{j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and that <math id="S5.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}_{X,Y}\left[\bigcap_{i,j}E_{i,j}^{c}\right]\leq\zeta_{k}+\xi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ζ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}_{X,Y}\left[\bigcap_{i,j}E_{i,j}^{c}\right]\leq\zeta_{k}+\xi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For details, see appendix D. We can now pull the upper bound on our inner expectation outside the sum over <math id="S5.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S5.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{s\in S^{\prime}}P(s)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{s\in S^{\prime}}P(s)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we get our desired result.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> is a CKDDG and suppose that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> are such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{l}(s)|\leq n,|C_{w}(s)|\geq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{l}(s)|\leq n,|C_{w}(s)|\geq m</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a policy whose error is bounded by a function of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> which decays faster than the reciprocal of any polynomial.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let’s define <math id="S5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\frac{m}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\frac{m}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. If we apply Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> with <math id="S5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1,k=l=1,\mu_{1}=\nu_{1}=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1,k=l=1,\mu_{1}=\nu_{1}=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (note that we need <math id="S5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure <math id="S5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}\geq\frac{\alpha}{\log_{2}(r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}\geq\frac{\alpha}{\log_{2}(r)}</annotation></semantics></math>) and simplify then we get an upper bound of <math id="S5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{-\frac{\sqrt{r}}{2}}+\left(\frac{e}{2}\right)^{-\frac{r}{2}}+\frac{1}{e}r^{-\log_{4}\left(\frac{\log_{4}(r)}{e}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mi>r</mi></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{-\frac{\sqrt{r}}{2}}+\left(\frac{e}{2}\right)^{-\frac{r}{2}}+\frac{1}{e}r^{-\log_{4}\left(\frac{\log_{4}(r)}{e}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> which decays faster than the reciprocal of any polynomial.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We now turn our attention to PIDDG’s. Here we can again find good policies for broad classes of problems, though the error does not decay as dramatically as with CKDDG’s. To understand the construction, suppose there is a scenario in a PIDDG where the desired loser has access to <math id="S5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> actions and the desired winner has access to <math id="S5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> actions. The principal can randomly rank all agent-action pairs and declare that whichever agent plays an action with a higher agent-action ranking wins. Whichever agent has an available action with the highest agent-action ranking among all available actions can guarantee themselves a win. The probability that the desired loser is this agent is <math id="S5.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{100}{100+10,000}=\frac{1}{101}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>10,000</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>101</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{100}{100+10,000}=\frac{1}{101}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this reasoning only requires the desired winner to know the ranking from the policy, not the desired loser’s available actions. Let’s now state and prove the general version:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Any PIDDG <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> has a policy with error at most <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\begin{cases}\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)\neq\emptyset\\
\frac{1}{2}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)=\emptyset\end{cases}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\begin{cases}\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)\neq\emptyset\\
\frac{1}{2}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)=\emptyset\end{cases}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">As before, let’s consider a random family of policies and argue that the average error of our policies is at most our desired bound. Let’s uniformly randomly select an injection <math id="S5.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma:\{1,2\}\times A\to\{1,...,2|A|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma:\{1,2\}\times A\to\{1,...,2|A|\}</annotation></semantics></math> (in other words, rank the agent-action pairs). Define <math id="S5.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}:\{1,2\}\times(A\cup\{\delta\})\to\{0,...,2|A|\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}:\{1,2\}\times(A\cup\{\delta\})\to\{0,...,2|A|\}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(i,a)=\begin{cases}\sigma(i,a)&amp;a\in A\\
0&amp;a=\delta\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(i,a)=\begin{cases}\sigma(i,a)&amp;a\in A\\
0&amp;a=\delta\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s define the policy <math id="S5.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\sigma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\sigma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that if agent <math id="S5.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> plays <math id="S5.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and if agent <math id="S5.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> plays <math id="S5.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S5.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\sigma^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\sigma^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> rules in favor of whichever agent has a higher value of <math id="S5.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(i,a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(i,a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> (and rewards both with probability <math id="S5.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(1,a_{1})=\sigma^{\prime}(2,a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(1,a_{1})=\sigma^{\prime}(2,a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. if <math id="S5.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}=a_{2}=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}=a_{2}=\delta</annotation></semantics></math>). Note that the optimal behavior for an agent does not depend on the other agent’s strategy or available actions.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">To evaluate the average error of such a policy, let’s do the same trick of exchanging the order of our expectations: first select an anonymous scenario <math id="S5.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> and an ordering of the agents, then determine the probability that a random policy will reward the wrong agent. If neither agent has any non-trivial actions then the desired loser has a <math id="S5.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> chance of winning, otherwise there will be <math id="S5.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|</annotation></semantics></math> valid agent-action pairs of which <math id="S5.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{l}(s)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{l}(s)|</annotation></semantics></math> belong to the desired loser so there is a <math id="S5.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}</annotation></semantics></math> chance that the desired loser ends up with the highest-ranked agent-action pair. Thus the expected error of a policy in this family is exactly <math id="S5.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\begin{cases}\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)\neq\emptyset\\
\frac{1}{2}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)=\emptyset\end{cases}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\begin{cases}\frac{|C_{l}(s)|}{|C_{w}(s)|+|C_{l}(s)|}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)\neq\emptyset\\
\frac{1}{2}&amp;C_{w}(s)\cup C_{l}(s)=\emptyset\end{cases}\right]</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that some policy has an error this low.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.4</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bound from Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> also holds for CKDDG’s and the proof is identical. However, Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> gives us an asymptotically weaker result than Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p13.1" class="ltx_p">We might hope that we could improve Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 5.3. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> with a cleverer technique to approach the faster-than-reciprocal-of-any-polynomial decay of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>. Unfortunately this is not possible in full generality: we can find a PIDDG such that the minimum error decays proportionally to the inverse of the ratio of the number of available actions for the desired winner to the number of available actions for the desired loser.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,n\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,n\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there exists a PIDDG <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(A,S,P,C_{w},C_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{w}(s)|\geq m,|C_{l}(s)|\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{w}(s)|\geq m,|C_{l}(s)|\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{B}^{\text{PIDDG}}(M)\geq\frac{n}{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>B</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">PIDDG</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{B}^{\text{PIDDG}}(M)\geq\frac{n}{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> for all policies <math id="S5.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=\{1,...,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=\{1,...,m\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\begin{pmatrix}A\\
n\end{pmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>A</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>n</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\begin{pmatrix}A\\
n\end{pmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s fix <math id="S5.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to be uniform over <math id="S5.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and let’s define <math id="S5.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w}(s)=A,C_{l}(s)=s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w}(s)=A,C_{l}(s)=s</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S5.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in S</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S5.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a policy for this PIDDG.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p15" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\text{PIDDG}}_{B}(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>B</mi><mtext>PIDDG</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\text{PIDDG}}_{B}(M)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text"> is the average of two quantities: the probability that agent <math id="S5.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> wins while being the desired loser and the probability that agent <math id="S5.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> wins while being the desired loser. For the first case, suppose <math id="S5.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{l}:S\to(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{l}:S\to(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the mixed strategy of agent <math id="S5.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in a given scenario and suppose <math id="S5.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}^{w}:(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}^{w}:(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the mixed strategy of agent <math id="S5.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> – note that agent <math id="S5.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> always gets every action and thus cannot infer anything about which scenario is unfolding. Suppose agent <math id="S5.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’s strategy is only supported on actions that they are permitted to use. Then the average win rate for agent <math id="S5.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(q_{1}^{l}(s)\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}\right]=\left(\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{1}^{l}(s)\right]\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[\left(q_{1}^{l}(s)\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}\right]=\left(\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{1}^{l}(s)\right]\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. Likewise, if agent <math id="S5.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired loser then they will win with probability <math id="S5.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{2}^{l}(s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{2}^{l}(s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{w},q_{2}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{w},q_{2}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined symmetrically to <math id="S5.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{2}^{w},q_{1}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{2}^{w},q_{1}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, let’s construct some strategies for the desired losers to lower bound the error that they can guarantee. Momentarily pretend that we’re in a new scenario where agents <math id="S5.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> have access to every possible action and define <math id="S5.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*}:(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*}:(A\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> to be resulting Nash equilibrium strategies. Now, for every <math id="S5.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> define <math id="S5.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="S5.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{l}(s,a)=q_{i}^{*}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{l}(s,a)=q_{i}^{*}(a)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{l}(s,a)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{l}(s,a)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S5.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A\setminus s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A\setminus s</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}^{l}(s,\delta)=q_{i}^{*}(\delta)+\sum_{b\in A\setminus s}q_{i}^{*}(b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}^{l}(s,\delta)=q_{i}^{*}(\delta)+\sum_{b\in A\setminus s}q_{i}^{*}(b)</annotation></semantics></math>. For every action <math id="S5.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in A</annotation></semantics></math>, exactly <math id="S5.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of the scenarios include <math id="S5.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="S5.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{i}^{l}(s,a)\right]=\frac{n}{m}q_{i}^{*}(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{i}^{l}(s,a)\right]=\frac{n}{m}q_{i}^{*}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>. We clearly have <math id="S5.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{i}^{l}(s,\delta)\right]\geq q_{i}^{*}(\delta)\geq\frac{n}{m}q_{i}^{*}(\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{i}^{l}(s,\delta)\right]\geq q_{i}^{*}(\delta)\geq\frac{n}{m}q_{i}^{*}(\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> so:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{1}^{l}(s)\right]\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{1}^{l}(s)\right]\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\left(\frac{n}{m}q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\left(\frac{n}{m}q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{2}^{l}(s)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\underset{s\sim P}{\mathbb{E}}\left[q_{2}^{l}(s)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\frac{n}{m}q_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)\frac{n}{m}q_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, since <math id="S5.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> are Nash equilibria we have:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>w</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(q_{1}^{w}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the error of <math id="S5.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is at least:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\frac{n}{m}\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}+\frac{n}{m}\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}}{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\frac{n}{m}\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(1,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}+\frac{n}{m}\left(q_{1}^{*}\right)^{\intercal}M(2,\cdot,\cdot)q_{2}^{*}}{2}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{n}{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{n}{2m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p16.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p16.4.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p17" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Although the details of this example are inconsequential, the proof technique generalizes: we may take a debate game, consider a new scenario which isn’t present in the given debate game, examine the strategies that the agents would adopt if they were faced with this hypothetical scenario, and then understand the behavior of the agents in the real scenarios in terms of their hypothetical behavior in this hypothetical scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.6</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We might ask whether we can attain a lower bound similar to Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 5.5. ‣ 5 Error Bounds ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> in the CKDDG case. Indeed we can: see appendix E.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Future research</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results could be generalized in a number of directions. For example, many of our results apply to distinguishing debate games, but there are likely analogues in settings where the two agents do not have the same set of actions. One could also consider debates between three or more agents. Additionally, one could consider trying to optimize debate in a more iterated setting where the principal wishes to minimize the cost of running the debate protocol by terminating it as early as possible. Another natural question is whether there are any special cases of debate games where the principal can compute an optimal policy in polynomial time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One particularly interesting direction involves looking at situations in which the principal cannot enforce whatever policy they wish. For example, suppose agents <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> are playing an extensive-form perfect information game where agent <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> either moves left or right and then agent <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> either moves left or right.
For any fixed payoffs at the leaves of this extensive-form game, we can convert this extensive-form game into a normal-form game where agent <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> has two actions and agent <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> has four actions. In this case, the map from choices of utilities at the leaves of our extensive-form game to payoff matrices in the normal-form game is not surjective. For example, the principal cannot reward agent <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> if agent <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> goes left and agent <math id="S6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> always goes left but also reward agent <math id="S6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> if agent <math id="S6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> goes left and agent <math id="S6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> does whatever agent <math id="S6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> did: agent <math id="S6.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> changed their action in the normal-form game (i.e. their pure strategy in the extensive-form game) but only in a way that differs on a branch that the principal never sees. Given that in many real-world games the principal cannot enforce whatever policy they wish, the question remains as to whether error bounds can be proven under such constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another particularly interesting direction involves examining debate games which are too big to specify. Are there natural ways to concisely represent debate games, especially in settings where the number of actions and/or scenarios is exponential in some other parameter? In this case, even our algorithms for <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">evaluating</span> a policy will require exponential time. What are good algorithms for reasoning about these representations? Let’s draw particular attention to the plight of the agents when handed a compressed representation of a policy. When can the principal expect the agents to respond near-optimally? All of these questions merit further examination.</p>
</div>
</section>
