<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>The payoffs for agent <math id="A1.T2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (represented as the row player) under the policy <math id="A1.T2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> referenced in the proof of Proposition 3.6.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let’s now show that every policy mapping to <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> has an error of at least <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.056" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.056</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.056</annotation></semantics></math>. To do this, suppose <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}:\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}^{2}\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}:\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}^{2}\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is arbitrary. Let <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,4\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,4\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the de-anonymized scenarios in the CKDG induced by our CKDDG.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">I first claim that if <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> has error less than <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{40}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}(1,\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}(1,\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> must take on the values listed in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2 Proposition 3.6 Proof Completion ‣ Appendix A Proof Completions of Initial Observations ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. To see why, note that if the error is less than <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{40}</annotation></semantics></math> then for every de-anonymized scenario in <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> the desired winner must have an action to play which wins against both actions the desired loser can play (for anonymized scenarios <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, both sides get a single non-default action). If this were not the case then for both of the desired winner’s actions, at least one action of the desired loser would favor the desired loser. The desired loser could then play each of their actions with <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> probability to guarantee an error of at least <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{3}{20}=\frac{3}{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{3}{20}=\frac{3}{40}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
