</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>If <math id="A1.T3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> has error less than <math id="A1.T3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{40}</annotation></semantics></math> then the payoffs for agent <math id="A1.T3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> (represented as the row player) must take on the values listed.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Next, I claim that for every de-anonymized scenario, this action of the desired winner which beats both of the desired loser’s actions must be the non-default action. To see why, again suppose towards a contradiction that in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,i)</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\{1,2\},i\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\{1,2\},i\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> favors agent <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> when agent <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> plays <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and when agent <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-w</annotation></semantics></math> plays either <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, in the de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-w,(i-1)\mod 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>mod</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-w,(i-1)\mod 3)</annotation></semantics></math>, agent <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> (now the desired loser) can again play <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> to guarantee a win, thus resulting in an error of at least <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{20}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus for de-anonymized scenarios <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> must favor the desired winner whenever the desired winner plays their non-default action. Thus <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> must be of the form depicted in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2 Proposition 3.6 Proof Completion ‣ Appendix A Proof Completions of Initial Observations ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> if the error of <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{40}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, conditional on <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> having the form described in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2 Proposition 3.6 Proof Completion ‣ Appendix A Proof Completions of Initial Observations ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, I claim that the error of <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.056" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.056</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.056</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, let’s partition the other de-anonymized scenarios into <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}=\{(1,3),(2,4)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}=\{(1,3),(2,4)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}=\{(2,3),(1,4)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}=\{(2,3),(1,4)\}</annotation></semantics></math>. I claim that the contribution to the error from <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.028" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.028</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.028</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, let’s case on the values of:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=M_{\{0,1\}}(1,\delta,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=M_{\{0,1\}}(1,\delta,3)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max(M_{\{0,1\}}(1,0,3),M_{\{0,1\}}(1,1,3),M_{\{0,1\}}(1,2,3))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max(M_{\{0,1\}}(1,0,3),M_{\{0,1\}}(1,1,3),M_{\{0,1\}}(1,2,3))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> then agent <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can always win de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> by playing action <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the error from <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{100}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> then agent <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can win with probability at least <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> by uniformly mixing over actions <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, agent <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can guarantee a win outright in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math> by playing one of the actions in <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> that caused <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the error from <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{100}+\frac{2}{100}=\frac{3}{100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{3}\cdot\frac{3}{100}+\frac{2}{100}=\frac{3}{100}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=0</annotation></semantics></math> then agent <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can win de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at least <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{5}</annotation></semantics></math> by playing actions <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{5}</annotation></semantics></math> each and playing action <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{2}{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{2}{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Meanwhile, agent <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can win de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,4)</annotation></semantics></math> with probability at least <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by playing action <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and action <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> with probability <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the error from <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{5}\cdot\frac{3}{100}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{100}=\frac{28}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{5}\cdot\frac{3}{100}+\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{2}{100}=\frac{28}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> then the analysis is identical to the case where <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0,y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0,y=1</annotation></semantics></math> and the error from <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{100}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{100}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">We thus see that the error from <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{28}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{28}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math> in all cases. An entirely symmetric analysis shows that the error from <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also at least <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{28}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>28</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{28}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the overall error is at least <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{56}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>56</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{56}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We have thus shown that if the error of <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{3}{40}=\frac{75}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>75</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{3}{40}=\frac{75}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\{0,1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\{0,1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> must resemble Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.2 Proposition 3.6 Proof Completion ‣ Appendix A Proof Completions of Initial Observations ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and thus have error at least <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{56}{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>56</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{56}{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>, showing that no policy mapping to <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> can have error below <math id="A1.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.056" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.056</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.056</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Proposition 3.7 Proof Completion</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show that our CKDDG has a policy whose error is strictly less than the minimum error among policies which incentivize pure strategies, again consider the policy <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> described in Table <a href="#A1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ A.2 Proposition 3.6 Proof Completion ‣ Appendix A Proof Completions of Initial Observations ‣ Designing Rules for Choosing a Winner in a Debate" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. As before, scenarios <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,1,2</annotation></semantics></math> contribute no error while in scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> the desired loser can win half of the time. Thus the error of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is again <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{10}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\cdot\frac{1}{10}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let’s show that every policy which incentivizes pure strategies has an error of at least <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{ps}}:\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}^{2}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>ps</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{ps}}:\{1,2\}\times\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}^{2}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a policy such that in every de-anonymized scenario there is a pure-strategy Nash equilibrium for the two agents. For each de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,s)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\{1,2\},s\in\{0,1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\{1,2\},s\in\{0,1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>, pick such a pure-strategy Nash equilibrium and define <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}(w,s)\in\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}(w,s)\in\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the action performed by agent <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> in this Nash equilibrium.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Now, let’s examine <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(1,3)\in\{0,1,2,\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(1,3)\in\{0,1,2,\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can always find some scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(1,3)\in C_{l}(s_{1})\cup\{\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(1,3)\in C_{l}(s_{1})\cup\{\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(1,3)\neq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(1,3)\neq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=b_{1}(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=b_{1}(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> works, if <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(1,3)=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(1,3)=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> then any <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> will suffice). We have <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w}(s_{1})\cup\{\delta\}\subseteq\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}=C_{l}(3)\cup\{\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w}(s_{1})\cup\{\delta\}\subseteq\{0,1,2,3,\delta\}=C_{l}(3)\cup\{\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math> so the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,s_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,s_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> (because in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,s_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,s_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can again play action <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> and agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>’s ability to respond is no better). A symmetric argument shows that we can find <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}\in\{0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}\in\{0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math> is at most the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,s_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,s_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{2}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{2}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the probability that agent <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> wins in de-anonymized scenario <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3)</annotation></semantics></math>. We have thus demonstrated that the error of <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{ps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>ps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{ps}}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\,(1-p_{1})\cdot\frac{1}{20}+p_{1}\cdot\frac{3}{20}+(1-p_{2})\cdot\frac{1}{20}+p_{2}\cdot\frac{3}{20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>20</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\,(1-p_{1})\cdot\frac{1}{20}+p_{1}\cdot\frac{3}{20}+(1-p_{2})\cdot\frac{1}{20}+p_{2}\cdot\frac{3}{20}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\,\frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{10}p_{1}+\frac{1}{10}p_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\,\frac{1}{10}+\frac{1}{10}p_{1}+\frac{1}{10}p_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≥</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\,\frac{1}{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\,\frac{1}{10}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Thus our result holds.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Hardness Proof Details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Lemma 4.5 Proof Completion</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let’s show that if there is a satisfying assignment then our CKDG has a perfect policy, that if our CKDG has a perfect policy then there is a satisfying assignment, that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, and that <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show that if there is a satisfying assignment then our CKDG has a perfect policy, suppose <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},...,x_{n}\in\{\bot,\top\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},...,x_{n}\in\{\bot,\top\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a satisfying assignment for <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}\land...\land K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}\land...\land K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the policy defined by:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(1,a_{1},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(1,a_{1},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1&amp;\exists i,j[a_{1}=(j,x_{j})\land a_{2}=(i,j)]\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1&amp;\exists i,j[a_{1}=(j,x_{j})\land a_{2}=(i,j)]\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the scenarios where we want agent <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to win (indexed by variables), agent <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> can take the action specified by that variable’s value in the satisfying assignment. In the scenarios where we want agent <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to win (indexed by clauses), agent <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> can take the action corresponding to the literal rendering that clause true. Thus <math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a perfect policy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show that if our CKDG has a perfect policy then there is a satisfying assignment, suppose <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a policy with zero error. Then for every scenario, there must be an action our desired winner can take such that the policy always favors them no matter the action by the desired loser (a <span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">perfect</span> action). Let <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}:\{1,...,n\}\to A_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}:\{1,...,n\}\to A_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote such a choice of perfect action for each of the scenarios in which agent <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner and let <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}:\{1,...,m\}\to A_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}:\{1,...,m\}\to A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the same for when agent <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner. For every <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> set <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{j}=\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}=\bot</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(j)=(j,\bot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊥</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(j)=(j,\bot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{j}=\top" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}=\top</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(j)=(j,\top)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊤</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(j)=(j,\top)</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> arbitrarily if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(j)=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(j)=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (note that these are our only options). I claim that we have found a satisfying assignment for <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}\land...\land K_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}\land...\land K_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see why, let’s examine <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="i\in\{1,..,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,..,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We must have <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}(i)\in(\{i\}\times J_{i})\cup\{\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}(i)\in(\{i\}\times J_{i})\cup\{\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}(i)=(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}(i)=(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> then variable <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> must make <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> true – we cannot have <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(j)=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(j)=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> because then <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> wouldn’t be a perfect action so we must have <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{1}(j)=(j,x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{1}(j)=(j,x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> so the only way for our policy to favor agent <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((j,x_{j}),(i,j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((j,x_{j}),(i,j))</annotation></semantics></math> but agent <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((j,\neg n_{i,j}),(i,j))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((j,\neg n_{i,j}),(i,j))</annotation></semantics></math> is if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}\neq\neg n_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}\neq\neg n_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}=n_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}=n_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> so <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K_{i}=\top" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">⊤</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}=\top</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{2}(i)=\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{2}(i)=\delta</annotation></semantics></math> then every variable must render <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> true because if a single one didn’t then again our perfect actions would conflict. Thus if <math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a policy with zero error then our formula is satisfiable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To see that <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, let’s start off by arguing that observing <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> allows us to determine whether agent <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is supposed to win in scenario <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. If agent <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> should win then their action set will only reference a single variable, but if agent <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> should win then their action set will reference at least two distinct variables. If agent <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> should win then we can determine the scenario by looking at which variable gets referenced. If agent <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> should win then our given action set allows us to determine the variables used in the clause and the state of their negations, which we’ve stated is enough to uniquely determine the clause. Thus <math id="A2.I1.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To see that <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, again note that given <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> we can determine which agent is the desired winner. If agent <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner then we will get exactly one action per clause but if agent <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner then we will get at least two actions per clause (clauses reference at least two variables and there is at least one clause). If agent <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner then we can look at which variable we’re getting access to in each clause. If agent <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired winner then we can look at which clause all of our actions are coming from. Thus <math id="A2.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is also injective.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Lemma 4.6 Proof Completion</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have four remaining tasks: showing that if the CKDG has a perfect policy then so does the CKDDG, showing that if the CKDDG has a perfect policy then so does the CKDG, and showing that <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{w},C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{w},C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> are injective.</p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show that if the CKDG has a perfect policy then so does the CKDDG, suppose <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M:\{1,2\}\times(A_{1}\cup\{\delta\})\times(A_{2}\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:\{1,2\}\times(A_{1}\cup\{\delta\})\times(A_{2}\cup\{\delta\})\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a perfect policy for our CKDG. Now, define <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\prime}:\{1,2\}\times(A\cup\{\delta\})^{2}\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\prime}:\{1,2\}\times(A\cup\{\delta\})^{2}\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> so that for all <math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},b_{1}\in A_{1}\cup\{\delta\},a_{2},b_{2}\in A_{2}\cup\{\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},b_{1}\in A_{1}\cup\{\delta\},a_{2},b_{2}\in A_{2}\cup\{\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),(1,b_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),(1,b_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),(2,a_{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),(2,a_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=M(1,a_{1},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=M(1,a_{1},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(1,a_{1}),\delta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(2,a_{2}),(1,a_{1}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M^{\prime}(1,(2,a_{2}),(1,a_{1}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=M(2,a_{1},a_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=M(2,a_{1},a_{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
